 "url": "http://engineering.atspotify.com/2026/4/background-coding-agents-dataset-migrations-honk-part-4",
 "verdicts": {
  "A context file generated by having Claude repurpose a human migration guide was insufficient and led Honk to make incorrect field-mapping assumptions; making mappings explicit in tables fixed this.": null,
  "At the time of the dataset migration, Honk had no access to Claude skills or custom configurability, a deliberate guardrail, so prompts had to be fully comprehensive since the agent could not use MCPs or read external docs.": null,
  "BigQuery Runner and dbt repositories rarely had build-time unit tests, so Honk's verify-and-adjust loop was unavailable and owning teams had to manually test the automated PRs before merging.": null,
  "In a Honk dataset-migration case study, Spotify needed to migrate about 1,800 direct downstream data pipelines in six months across three frameworks (BigQuery Runner, dbt, and Scala-based Scio), estimated at about 10 engineering weeks if done manually.": null,
  "Spotify dropped Scio migrations in this effort because pipelines varied too much between teams to write a comprehensive prompt, while the more standardized dbt and BigQuery Runner pipelines worked.": null,
  "Spotify instructed Honk to leave fields needing judgment calls unchanged and add comments linking to human migration guides for reviewers.": null,
  "Spotify rolled out 240 automated migration PRs via Fleetshift for the dataset migration, saving an estimated 10 engineering weeks.": null,
  "Spotify used Backstage endpoint lineage and Codesearch plugins to identify downstream consumers and target repositories for the dataset migration.": null
 }
}